WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:08.979
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:16.482
Unit twenty-three: Survey Design. Today you will link actions concisely with participle clauses.

4
00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:19.481
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.736
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:25.573
Survey.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:30.139
Survey means a systematic method of collecting data from a sample.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.226
The survey reached over two thousand households.

9
00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:36.713
Your turn. Say: Survey.

10
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:40.579
Survey.

11
00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:42.271
Questionnaire.

12
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:46.618
Questionnaire means a written set of questions used in a survey.

13
00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:49.808
The questionnaire took twelve minutes to complete.

14
00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:53.255
Your turn. Say: Questionnaire.

15
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.240
Questionnaire.

16
00:00:57.983 --> 00:00:58.892
Sample.

17
00:00:59.883 --> 00:01:03.299
Sample means a subset of a population selected for study.

18
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:06.591
The sample was stratified by region and age.

19
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:09.615
Your turn. Say: Sample.

20
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.305
Sample.

21
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.051
Bias.

22
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:20.546
Bias means a systematic distortion in how data is collected or interpreted.

23
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.916
Leading questions introduce response bias.

24
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.728
Your turn. Say: Bias.

25
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:30.626
Bias.

26
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:32.233
Scale.

27
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.900
Scale means a graded set of response options.

28
00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:38.738
A five-point scale measured agreement.

29
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.724
Your turn. Say: Scale.

30
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:45.545
Scale.

31
00:01:46.283 --> 00:01:47.517
Response rate.

32
00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:51.747
Response rate means the percentage of people who complete a survey.

33
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.429
A low response rate threatens representativeness.

34
00:01:56.017 --> 00:01:59.064
Your turn. Say: Response rate.

35
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.075
Response rate.

36
00:02:03.817 --> 00:02:04.713
Pilot.

37
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:09.829
Pilot means to test a research instrument on a small group first.

38
00:02:10.417 --> 00:02:13.078
We piloted the questionnaire with ten students.

39
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:16.359
Your turn. Say: Pilot.

40
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.004
Pilot.

41
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.041
Reliability.

42
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.861
Reliability means the consistency of results produced by an instrument.

43
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.682
Clear wording improves reliability.

44
00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:33.412
Your turn. Say: Reliability.

45
00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:37.349
Reliability.

46
00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:41.587
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:47.320
Visal, your survey on screen time changed a lot between versions. What happened?

48
00:02:48.417 --> 00:02:54.552
Having piloted the survey, the team revised the questions. Several turned out to be leading.

49
00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.034
Can you give an example?

50
00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:06.410
We originally asked, 'Don't you agree that phones are harmful?' Realising it pushed respondents towards 'yes', we rewrote it neutrally.

51
00:03:07.517 --> 00:03:09.725
And how did you improve the response rate?

52
00:03:10.817 --> 00:03:17.135
Shortening the questionnaire to ten minutes, we saw completion rise from forty to seventy percent.

53
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.963
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:23.513
Grammar focus: Participle clauses.

55
00:03:23.813 --> 00:03:25.000
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.683 --> 00:03:28.855
Having piloted the survey, the team revised the questions.

57
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:33.387
Realising the question was leading, we rewrote it neutrally.

58
00:03:34.283 --> 00:03:37.177
Shortening the questionnaire, we raised the response rate.

59
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:48.997
Participle clauses replace 'after / because / when + subject + verb'. 'Having + past participle' shows the action was completed first. The main subject must be the one doing both actions.

60
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:53.772
Be careful! Don't say: Having pilot the survey, we revised it.

61
00:03:55.733 --> 00:03:58.882
Say: Having piloted the survey, we revised it.

62
00:03:59.182 --> 00:04:01.460
Use 'having' + past participle.

63
00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:04.965
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:07.615
Asking the Right Questions.

65
00:04:07.915 --> 00:04:10.588
A survey is only as good as the questions it asks.

66
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.024
Poorly worded items can introduce bias, steering respondents towards particular answers.

67
00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:23.168
Having piloted the survey, the team revised the questions to remove ambiguity and leading phrasing.

68
00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:29.765
Recognising that long questionnaires discourage completion, designers increasingly keep surveys brief.

69
00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:35.924
A representative sample and a high response rate together determine whether the results can be trusted.

70
00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:40.989
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.790
blank the survey, the team revised the questions.

72
00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:49.324
A, Having piloted. B, Have piloted. C, Piloted having.

73
00:04:52.867 --> 00:04:55.052
The answer is A: Having piloted.

74
00:04:56.217 --> 00:04:59.099
Which participle clause is correct (same subject)?

75
00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:08.718
A, Having finished the survey, the data was analysed.. B, Having finished the survey, we analysed the data.. C, Finished the survey, we analysing data..

76
00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:16.263
The answer is B: Having finished the survey, we analysed the data..

77
00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:21.009
'Don't you agree that phones are harmful?' is a blank question.

78
00:05:21.309 --> 00:05:25.044
A, neutral. B, leading. C, closed-scale.

79
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.379
The answer is B: leading.

80
00:05:31.517 --> 00:05:34.387
The percentage of people who complete a survey is the blank.

81
00:05:34.687 --> 00:05:39.153
A, response rate. B, reliability. C, sample.

82
00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.839
The answer is A: response rate.

83
00:05:45.983 --> 00:05:49.399
According to the reading, why do designers keep surveys brief?

84
00:05:49.699 --> 00:05:55.744
A, Long ones discourage completion.. B, Short ones are cheaper to print.. C, Pilots require it..

85
00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.623
The answer is A: Long ones discourage completion..

86
00:06:03.833 --> 00:06:06.892
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:10.609
How can the wording of a question bias survey results?

88
00:06:27.417 --> 00:06:28.847
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:29.147 --> 00:07:00.406
The wording of a question can easily push respondents towards a particular answer. For example, asking 'How much do you enjoy our excellent service?' assumes the service is excellent and discourages criticism. Loaded words, double questions and assumptions can all distort the data. Having recognised these problems, careful researchers pilot their questions and rewrite them neutrally. Some bias is perhaps unavoidable, but thoughtful design can reduce it considerably.

90
00:07:00.706 --> 00:07:02.334
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.330
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle clauses.

92
00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:12.291
Today's value is consideration. Use a quiet voice when someone is sleeping.

93
00:07:12.591 --> 00:07:15.534
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:15.834 --> 00:07:19.032
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

